		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Friction in Cline's paper: how to use?]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=3041#p3041"><![CDATA[
The type of solver you need depends on your application. If you are willing to sacrifice accuracy for speed, you should consider sequential impulses.<br><br>AFAIK, solving an exact model of friction requires solving a Nolinear Complementarity Problem (NCP). You won't find many brave enough to tackle that problem.<br><br>With an iterative method like sequential impulses, you can easily use <em class="text-italics">weight feeling</em> friction.<br><br>BTW, artifacts of using a friction pyramid instead of a cone can be reduced by aligning the friction axes with the relative velocity.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=12">Erin Catto</a> — Sat Dec 30, 2006 6:48 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
